WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.500
In Virginia, some people call a vulture a buzzard.

2
00:00:08.180 --> 00:00:10.960
In Britain, a buzzard is a kind of hawk.

3
00:00:11.780 --> 00:00:13.240
So which bird do they mean?

4
00:00:14.120 --> 00:00:17.940
One common name can mean different birds in different places.

5
00:00:18.950 --> 00:00:24.230
Scientists fix this with one name for each kind of living thing.

6
00:00:25.190 --> 00:00:30.650
A species is a group of similar-looking organisms that can mate in nature and have

7
00:00:30.650 --> 00:00:32.510
young that can also reproduce.

8
00:00:33.510 --> 00:00:37.630
This idea works for many organisms that reproduce with sex cells.

9
00:00:38.290 --> 00:00:43.610
It does not fit organisms that reproduce without sex, and some different species do

10
00:00:43.610 --> 00:00:45.150
sometimes mate in nature.

11
00:00:45.150 --> 00:00:49.150
Each species gets a two-part scientific name.

12
00:00:50.033 --> 00:00:55.733
The first word of the name is the genus, a small group of closely related species.

13
00:00:56.633 --> 00:00:59.693
The second word names one species in that genus.

14
00:01:00.593 --> 00:01:03.213
The turkey vulture is Cathartes aura.

15
00:01:03.853 --> 00:01:06.553
The black vulture is Coragyps atratus.

16
00:01:07.693 --> 00:01:09.553
The genus gets a capital letter.

17
00:01:10.233 --> 00:01:11.893
The second word is lowercase.

18
00:01:13.053 --> 00:01:19.653
A scientist in Japan who writes Cathartes aura means the same bird you see in Virginia.

19
00:01:19.867 --> 00:01:27.567
Scientists also sort each species into eight levels, from most general to most specific.

20
00:01:28.167 --> 00:01:38.867
They are domain, kingdom, phylum, class, order, family, genus, and species.

21
00:01:39.117 --> 00:01:41.247
A domain is the largest group.

22
00:01:42.027 --> 00:01:46.647
There are three: Archaea, Bacteria, and Eukarya.

23
00:01:46.647 --> 00:01:50.127
Plants and animals are two kingdoms.

24
00:01:51.067 --> 00:01:52.947
Think of a mailing address.

25
00:01:53.747 --> 00:01:58.347
Country, state, city, and street each narrow the search.

26
00:01:59.187 --> 00:02:01.687
The levels of classification narrow it too.

27
00:02:02.747 --> 00:02:06.047
Organisms that share more levels are grouped more closely.

28
00:02:07.027 --> 00:02:12.447
Scientists decide the groups by comparing body parts, what organisms do, and DNA.

29
00:02:13.867 --> 00:02:16.367
Names can still change when scientists learn more.

30
00:02:17.150 --> 00:02:19.810
A dichotomous key is a different tool.

31
00:02:20.530 --> 00:02:25.850
It names an organism from traits you can see, one two-choice step at a time.

32
00:02:26.510 --> 00:02:29.030
It does not show which organisms are related.

33
00:02:29.870 --> 00:02:33.410
So how do scientists know they mean the same living thing?

34
00:02:34.170 --> 00:02:40.170
They use its scientific name, shared in every country, and its place in the eight levels.
